// ── ONE MODEL IN, A DEPLOYMENT SHAPE OR AN OBLIGATION OUT ─────────────────────────────────────
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS. Every deployment question this fleet has asked -- how many ranks, what context
// ceiling, what memory fraction, whether to take a faster quantisation -- has been answered by
// someone reading a benchmark and editing a launch script. That process has no terminating
// condition: there is always another flag, and nothing records why the last value was chosen or
// what would change it.
//
// THE REPLACEMENT IS A FUNCTION, NOT A BETTER BENCHMARK. Given an exact artifact, an evidence
// snapshot and a bounded choice domain, it derives the complete candidate field, attaches the
// evidence each axis actually has, and hands the choice to std.decision. It answers with a shape
// only when the evidence supports one, and otherwise names what is missing.
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE IS NOT. It is not a selector. std.decision.select_realization owns choosing and
// std.pareto owns dominance; DESIGN section 3d is explicit that growing a second decision algebra
// beside them is the fork that law exists to prevent. This module generates candidates, derives
// feasibility, and projects domain-typed readings into the generic scalar exactly once.
//
// AND IT IS NOT A LAUNCH. What it returns is a deployment SHAPE. Binding replica shapes to exact
// hosts, network groups, failure domains and endpoints is the fabric's relation, and only that
// relation may mint a launch. Two earlier versions of this module overstated their output and both
// overstatements were caught in review rather than by a wall; the vocabulary below is chosen so the
// third does not need catching.

// ── HOW A FOOTPRINT WAS OBTAINED IS PART OF WHAT IT MEANS ─────────────────────────────────────
//
// Weight footprint decides feasibility before any benchmark does, so it is the first thing that
// must be real. A figure from a model card or a third-party report is a citation about a checkpoint,
// not a reading of the bytes THIS FLEET will serve -- and the previous version collapsed both into
// one `FootprintMeasured` arm, so a number lifted from someone else's deployment write-up was typed
// identically to one read on a Spark. Its own `how` field said as much while its constructor name
// said "measured".
// FOUR PROVENANCES, NOT TWO, BECAUSE THE MIDDLE TWO ARE NOT THE SAME KIND OF THING. An earlier
// revision had one non-fleet arm and lumped together a stranger's deployment write-up and the
// publishing Hub's own file manifest at an exact content-addressed revision. Those differ in what
// they can support: a write-up cites a number someone else remembers, while a manifest at a pinned
// revision is the authority that serves the bytes stating their sizes, re-readable by anyone.
//
// WHAT THE UPSTREAM ARM MAY DO IS BOUNDED BY THE ASYMMETRY THE BOUND ALREADY HAS. Disproving
// feasibility needs only a floor on the size, and upstream's own manifest is a sound floor -- if the
// publisher says 305.8 GiB, no rank smaller than that share can hold it. Establishing feasibility was
// never something the bound could do from ANY footprint, so admitting this arm widens nothing: it
// still yields "not ruled out", and runtime feasibility remains a separate, unsatisfied axis.
type ArtifactFootprint
  = FootprintObservedOnFleet { bytes: Nat, how: NonEmptyStr }
  | FootprintDeclaredByUpstreamAtRevision { bytes: Nat, revision: NonEmptyStr, instrument: NonEmptyStr }
  | FootprintReportedExternally { bytes: Nat, source: NonEmptyStr }
  | FootprintUnread { obligation: NonEmptyStr }

type ModelArtifact {
  identity: DeclarationRef
  label: NonEmptyStr
  footprint: ArtifactFootprint
  weight_treatment: NonEmptyStr
}

data artifact_deepseek_v4_flash_native: ModelArtifact = ModelArtifact {
  identity: DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.spark.serving_deployment_selection", decl_name: "artifact_deepseek_v4_flash_native", field: WholeDeclaration },
